#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
#
# Installed by `make hooks`, never automatically: a repository that rewrites the
# developer's git configuration as a side effect of building is doing something they
# did not ask for.
#
# This runs the formatting check and the leak gate, and nothing else: together about
# 15 seconds on a loaded host. Lints and tests belong to `make check`, which a contributor
# runs deliberately and CI runs again; a hook that runs the full gate gets disabled the
# first time someone is in a hurry, and a disabled hook protects nothing. The boundary is
# the one thing that cannot be checked later, because a commit is where the object enters
# the graph; the formatting check is here because it costs a second and fails the gate
# minutes later otherwise.
#
# What is not here, and why (measured 2026-10-21 on Linux x86_64 at load 34 to 47): the
# text-scan ratchets (`make ratchets`) take 3 s with
# their test binaries built, but 10.5 s after any source change and 83 s on the first commit
# in a fresh worktree, which builds them; the hook admits a check only under about 10 s.
# fmt-check took 1.3 s and the leak gate 13.6 s (9 s near idle, with its one-pass read).

set -euo pipefail

if ! cargo fmt --check >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'

The commit was refused because the tree is not formatted: run `cargo fmt` and commit again.
`make merge-gate` would refuse it at its fmt-check phase.
EOF
    exit 1
fi

if ! scripts/leak-gate.sh; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'

The commit was refused because this tree would publish something it should not.

This is not a formatting rule. An object entering the graph here is reachable from a
ref, which means a clone receives it and a push transfers it, and removing it later
does not remove it from history. Fix it now, while it is still cheap.

There is deliberately no override flag. If the gate is wrong, fix the gate --
scripts/leak-gate.sh -- so the next person inherits the correction.
EOF
    exit 1
fi
